. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2 * 
. 
. /*      * Please add a verbal description of your model here: 
> 
> Our measure political preferences is based on respondents'  self-placement on
>  the left–right political spectrum, using a scale from 0 ("ganz links" / far 
> left) to 10 ("ganz rechts" / far right). Accordingly, we estimate a linear OL
> S regression where the dependent variable is plh0004, and the key independent
>  variable is a dummy, d_daughter, which takes the value 1 if the respondent h
> ad at least one daughter at the time of responding (survey year: 2019).
> 
> Our objective is to estimate the causal effect of having a daughter on gender
>  equality attitudes. While the gender of a child is generally considered to b
> e randomly assigned, fertility decisions are not. Therefore, we restrict our 
> sample to respondents who were already parents at the time of the survey.
> 
> However, it is important to acknowledge that subsequent fertility decisions m
> ay be influenced by the gender of previously born children, i.e. "gender stop
> ping rules" (Angrist and Evans, 1998). If such decisions are driven by gender
>  preferences, this could threaten the causal interpretation of our estimates.
>  We tested the possibility of gender stopping rules by regressing the number 
> of children on the gender of the first born. By doing so, we found no evidenc
> e of correlation (coef 0.008833, p-value=0.848), indicating that gender stopp
> ing rules are not extended among the participants. Additionally, to mitigate 
> this concern, we control for the number of children the respondent had at the
>  time of completing the questionnaire. 
> 
> We include the following controls: participant gender, net monthly income of 
> the household, a dummy for german born participants, and birth year of the pa
> rticipant fixed effects. 
> 
> */
.         
.  
. 
. 
. 
.  
. reg plh0004 d_daughter  i.sex  i.germborn hghinc  i.gebjahr sumkids_dyanamic 
